<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-general-solution-nonhomogeneous" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The General Solution</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXPLORATION: BUILDING THE GENERAL SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exploration xml:id="exploration-general-solution">
    <title>Finding the General Solution to a Non-Homogeneous Equation</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Our goal is to find the <em>general solution</em> to the non-homogeneous equation
        <me>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t).</me>
        To do this, it turns out we need two ingredients: a <term>particular solution</term> <m>y_p</m>
        to the non-homogeneous equation, and the <term>complementary function</term> <m>y_c</m>,
        which is the general solution to the associated homogeneous equation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point of this exploration is to see why the sum <m>y_p + y_c</m> will always form a solution to the
        non-homogeneous ODE. </p>
        <p>
        <alert>You can skim the computations for <m>y_p</m> and <m>y_c</m>, but pay close attention to parts
        (c) and (d)!</alert>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ── Part (a): Find yp ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-gensol-find-yp">
      <title>Find a particular solution <m>y_p</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the method of undetermined coefficients to find a particular solution <m>y_p</m> to
          <me>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t).</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Answer:</alert>
          <me>y_p = -\frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t).</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <title>Full computation (can skim)</title>
        <p>
          The right-hand side has frequency <m>k = 2</m>, and the coefficient of <m>y</m>
          is <m>2 \neq k^2 = 4</m>, so this is the normal case. We guess
          <me>y_p = A\cos(2t) + B\sin(2t).</me>
          Computing derivatives:
          <md>
            <mrow>y_p' \amp= -2A\sin(2t) + 2B\cos(2t)</mrow>
            <mrow>y_p'' \amp= -4A\cos(2t) - 4B\sin(2t)</mrow>
          </md>
          Substituting into <m>y'' - 3y' + 2y</m> and collecting:
          <md>
            <mrow>\amp(-4A\cos(2t) - 4B\sin(2t))</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp\quad - 3(-2A\sin(2t) + 2B\cos(2t))</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp\quad + 2(A\cos(2t) + B\sin(2t))</mrow>
            <mrow>= \amp(-2A - 6B)\cos(2t) + (6A - 2B)\sin(2t).</mrow>
          </md>
          Matching coefficients with the right-hand side <m>2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t)</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>-2A - 6B \amp= 2</mrow>
            <mrow>6A - 2B \amp= -4</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <solution>
          <title>Solving the system (can skim)</title>
          <p>
            Multiply the first equation by <m>3</m> and add to the second:
            <md>
              <mrow>-6A - 18B \amp= 6</mrow>
              <mrow>6A - 2B \amp= -4</mrow>
              <mrow>\hline -20B \amp= 2 \implies B = -\tfrac{1}{10}.</mrow>
            </md>
            Substituting back: <m>6A - 2(-\tfrac{1}{10}) = -4 \implies 6A = -4 - \tfrac{1}{5} = -\tfrac{21}{5} \implies A = -\tfrac{7}{10}.</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
        <p>
          So the particular solution is
          <me>y_p = -\frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t).</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Part (b): Find yc ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-gensol-find-yc">
      <title>Find the complementary function <m>y_c</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution <m>y_c</m> to the associated homogeneous equation
          <me>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0.</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Answer:</alert>
          <me>y_c = c_1 e^{t} + c_2 e^{2t}.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <title>Full computation (can skim; see <xref ref="sec-characteristic-equation"/>)</title>
        <p>
          We solved equations like this in <xref ref="sec-characteristic-equation"/>.
          The characteristic equation is
          <me>r^2 - 3r + 2 = 0 \implies (r-1)(r-2) = 0 \implies r = 1,\ 2.</me>
          Two distinct real roots give the general solution
          <me>y_c = c_1 e^{t} + c_2 e^{2t}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Part (c): Show yc + yp is a solution ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-gensol-verify">
      <title>Checking that <m>y_c + y_p</m> is a solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now let's verify that <m>y = y_c + y_p</m> actually satisfies the
          non-homogeneous equation. Substituting into the left-hand side and
          grouping the <m>y_c</m> and <m>y_p</m> terms together:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp (y_c + y_p)'' - 3(y_c + y_p)' + 2(y_c + y_p)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              = \amp \underbrace{(y_c'' - 3y_c' + 2y_c)}_{= \, 0}
                + \underbrace{(y_p'' - 3y_p' + 2y_p)}_{= \, 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t)}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              = \amp \; 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t). \checkmark
            </mrow>
          </md>
          The <m>y_c</m> group is zero because <m>y_c</m> solves the homogeneous equation;
          the <m>y_p</m> group equals the right-hand side because <m>y_p</m> is a particular solution.
          So <m>y_c + y_p</m> is a solution for <em>any</em> values of <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Part (d): MC checkpoint ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-gensol-mc">
      <title>The key idea</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Looking back at the verification above, why does <m>y_c + y_p</m> solve
          the non-homogeneous equation <m>y'' - 3y' + 2y = 2\cos(2t) - 4\sin(2t)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              When you plug in <m>y_c + y_p</m>, the <m>y_c</m> part cancels to zero
              (since it solves the homogeneous equation), and the <m>y_p</m> part
              produces exactly the right-hand side (since it's a particular solution).
              The sum is therefore equal to the right-hand side.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Exactly! The two pieces play complementary roles: <m>y_p</m> does the
              job of matching the right-hand side, while <m>y_c</m> quietly disappears.
              <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Both <m>y_c</m> and <m>y_p</m> individually solve the non-homogeneous
              equation, so their sum does too.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. <m>y_c</m> solves the <em>homogeneous</em> equation
              (right-hand side zero), not the non-homogeneous one. It's specifically
              because they solve <em>different</em> equations that the sum works.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> can always be chosen to make
              any function satisfy the equation.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> give flexibility, but they
              don't make an arbitrary function a solution. The reason <m>y_c + y_p</m>
              works is specifically because of what <m>y_c</m> and <m>y_p</m> each solve,
              not because of the free constants.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Adding two functions always produces a solution to the original equation.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This isn't true in general. The sum of two random functions won't
              usually satisfy a given ODE. The key is that <m>y_c</m> and <m>y_p</m>
              are carefully chosen to solve related equations whose contributions
              add up to the right-hand side.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

  </exploration>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BRIDGE PROSE: WRITE OUT GENERAL SOLUTION + CONSTANTS NOTE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    So we've shown that <m>y_c + y_p</m> is always a solution. For our example, that gives the general solution
    <me>y = \underbrace{c_1 e^{t} + c_2 e^{2t}}_{y_c} + \underbrace{\left(-\frac{7}{10}\cos(2t) - \frac{1}{10}\sin(2t)\right)}_{y_p}.</me>
    The two free constants <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> live entirely in <m>y_c</m> and can be
    determined by initial conditions, just as in the homogeneous case. The particular solution
    <m>y_p</m> is a single fixed function with no free parameters; it just does the job of
    matching the right-hand side.
  </p>
  <p>
    In fact, <m>y_c + y_p</m> turns out to be <em>all</em> solutions. That is, every solution
    to the non-homogeneous equation can be written in this form. Showing this rigorously requires
    more theory regarding existence and uniqueness of solutions to 2nd order linear ODEs than we
    present in this course, so we'll take it as given and state the result:
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THEOREM: GENERAL SOLUTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-general-solution-nonhomogeneous">
    <title>General Solution to the Non-Homogeneous Equation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>y_p</m> is a particular solution to
        <me>ay'' + by' + cy = g(t),</me>
        and <m>y_c</m> is the general solution to the associated homogeneous equation
        <me>ay'' + by' + cy = 0.</me>
        Then the general solution to <m>ay'' + by' + cy = g(t)</m> is
        <me>y = y_c + y_p.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Two parts of the general solution:</alert> To solve a non-homogeneous equation,
          find <m>y_p</m> (one particular solution, using undetermined coefficients)
          and <m>y_c</m> (the general solution to the associated homogeneous equation,
          using the characteristic equation). The general solution is <m>y = y_c + y_p</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Why it works:</alert> When you substitute <m>y_c + y_p</m>,
          the <m>y_c</m> terms cancel to zero and the <m>y_p</m> terms produce
          the right-hand side, so the sum satisfies the full non-homogeneous equation.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

